               4
            ],
            3
         ],
         2
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = 3 x + 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Multiply",
               3,
               "x"
            ],
            1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then \\( du = 3 \\, dx \\), so \\( dx = \\frac{1}{3} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            3
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( F(5) = F(1) + \\int_{1}^{5} \\frac{1}{3 x + 1} \\, dx \\), convert the limits: \\( x = 1 \\) gives \\( u = 4 \\) and \\( x = 5 \\) gives \\( u = 16 \\), so the integral becomes \\( \\int_{4}^{16} \\frac{1}{3 u} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Multiply",
               3,
               "u"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "An antiderivative of \\( \\frac{1}{3 u} \\) is \\( \\frac{\\ln{\\left(u \\right)}}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Ln",
               "u"
            ],
            3
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 5,
         "text": "The integral is \\( \\frac{\\ln{\\left(16 \\right)}}{3} - \\left(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{3} \\), so \\( F(5) = 2 + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{3} = \\frac{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}{3} + 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
